 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in relation to the penalty for unlawfully fleeing a uniformed officer of the New York city taxi and limousine commission",
    "basePrintNo": "S6202",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6202-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §19-506, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6202         REVISED MEMO 01/16/2016\n\nSPONSOR: LANZA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the administrative code of the city of\nNew York, in relation to the penalty for unlawfully fleeing a uniformed\nofficer of the New York city taxi and limousine commission\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe proposed legislation would amend subdivision 1 of section 19-506 of\nthe Administrative Code to broaden the misdemeanor crime of fleeing so\nthat it applies to any driver of a vehicle licensed by the Taxi and\nLimousine Commission (\"TLC\") who flees or attempts to flee when stopped\nby a police officer or by a TLC uniformed officer.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nFrequently, when a uniformed TLC inspector attempts to stop a vehicle\nengaged in for-hire transportation for an observed or suspected\nviolation of law, the driver of the vehicle simply flees the scene.\nSince TLC inspectors are not armed or authorized to give chase, this is\na risk-free way for drivers to avoid enforcement action. The Administra-\ntive Code presently punishes fleeing a police officer or a TLC inspector\nas a misdemeanor crime, in a provision enacted by the Legislature as\npart of the legislation that established street hail livery vehicles in\n2012. However, this crime is limited to street hail livery drivers who\nflee when stopped in a zone in which they are prohibited from picking up\nstreet hails.\n\nThe proposed legislation would change this provision so that it applies\nto the driver of any TLC-licensed vehicle who flees when stopped by a\npolice officer or TLC inspector. This would make it more likely that all\ndrivers subject to TLC's jurisdiction would obey a TLC stop, enhancing\nthe enforcement of TLC's regulatory system and preventing the prolifer-\nation of unlicensed drivers. Accordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest\npossible favorable consideration of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017: S.6202 - Died on Third Reading Calendar\n2016: S.5723A - Passed Senate / A.8190A - Referred to Codes.\n2015: S.5723A - Passed Senate / A.8190A - Reported to Rules.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
